# krabka-broker Bazel configuration.
#
# Cargo remains the dependency source of truth; Bazel reads the same
# Cargo.toml / Cargo.lock through rules_rs (see MODULE.bazel).

# Keep the action environment hermetic so build-script outputs are reproducible.
build --incompatible_strict_action_env

# The @llvm toolchain passes `--unwindlib=none` on link actions deliberately, to
# stop clang picking a libunwind for itself. clang then reports the argument as
# unused, and rustc's `linker_messages` lint surfaces that on stderr -- one
# warning per link, which is most of a CI log. The toolchain already suppresses
# the same complaint for its header-parsing and LTO actions and carries a TODO
# about rules_cc passing extra args; this extends that to linking.
build --linkopt=-Wno-unused-command-line-argument
# The same suppression for the exec configuration. Tools built for the build
# itself -- process_wrapper among them -- link under that configuration, which
# `--linkopt` does not reach, so the warning survived there alone.
build --host_linkopt=-Wno-unused-command-line-argument

# Keep the autodetected host C++ toolchain from outranking the hermetic @llvm one
# registered in MODULE.bazel.
common --repo_env=BAZEL_DO_NOT_DETECT_CPP_TOOLCHAIN=1
common --repo_env=BAZEL_NO_APPLE_CPP_TOOLCHAIN=1

test --test_output=errors

# Clippy, as an aspect over the targets the build already has.
#
#     bazel build --config=lint //...    # report
#     bazel test //...                   # fail on a violation
#
# It sees the crates, features and dependencies the build sees, rather than a
# second `cargo clippy` resolution that can disagree with it.
build:lint --aspects=//tools/lint:linters.bzl%clippy
build:lint --output_groups=rules_lint_report
# `clippy::pedantic` is a warning by workspace policy, and CLAUDE.md forbids
# suppressing it. Warnings therefore have to fail, or the policy is decorative.
test --@aspect_rules_lint//lint:fail_on_violation

# Coverage. rules_rust emits LLVM source-based coverage, which Bazel merges into
# one LCOV file at bazel-out/_coverage/_coverage_report.dat.
#
#     bazel coverage //crates/...
#
# Only first-party crates are instrumented: third-party coverage is noise, and
# instrumenting the whole dependency closure is most of the run.
coverage --combined_report=lcov
coverage --instrumentation_filter=^//crates[:/]
# Profiling instrumentation slows every test enough to trip the wall-clock
# assertions in the consensus and replication suites -- they pass in the test
# run and fail here, which is a statement about the instrumentation rather than
# the code. They gate in the `ci` job's `test` step
# (`bazel test --test_tag_filters=timing-sensitive //crates/...`), not here;
# this run exists for the report.
# Both exclusions, because `--test_tag_filters` is last-wins rather than
# additive: naming only `-timing-sensitive` here replaced the `-docker` set for
# `test` above, and the coverage run started trying to drive containers.
coverage --test_tag_filters=-docker,-timing-sensitive
# `--test_tag_filters` excludes a test from the *run*, not from the build, so
# without this the excluded suites are still linked. That is not free here: each
# one is a separately linked, instrumented copy of the whole broker, and the 22
# `*_docker_test` wrappers each drag in a second copy of their suite as a
# `*_docker_bin`. Building the 27 suites this run never executes is what filled
# the CI runner's disk. `--build_tests_only` narrows the build to the tests the
# filters above leave behind; the shippable non-test targets are covered by the
# separate `bazel build` in CI.
coverage --build_tests_only

# Download a JDK rather than resolving the machine's. `jvm_tiered_storage` runs
# a small Java probe against Kafka's own storage classes; without this the
# toolchain resolves to `local_jdk` and fails on a machine with no JDK, which is
# how that suite fails under Cargo today.
build --java_runtime_version=remotejdk_21

# Container-driven suites are opt-in. They start real Kafka brokers through
# testcontainers, so they need a Docker daemon and several hundred megabytes of
# images; `bazel test //...` on a machine without one should not fail.
#
#     bazel test --config=docker //...
test --test_tag_filters=-docker
test:docker --test_tag_filters=docker

# Remote build execution, opt in per invocation with `--config=rbe`.
#
#     bazel build --config=rbe //...
#
# Off by default and named per step rather than per job, which is what keeps the
# `timing-sensitive` suites on a real runner: they assert on wall clock and are
# already `flaky = True`, and `cpu:4` -- the tag that stops Bazel starving them
# locally -- means nothing to a remote executor, which sizes actions from
# `exec_properties` instead.
#
# The execution platform is the one the `llvm` module already publishes rather
# than a hand-written copy. That matters for more than tidiness: it carries
# `@llvm//constraints/libc:gnu.2.28` beside the os and cpu constraints, and
# without that constraint the C++ toolchain does not resolve on the executor at
# all. It also pins `docker://ubuntu:22.04`, chosen by the people who build the
# toolchain to match the glibc it targets. A platform written here would have to
# track both, and would silently rot when the toolchain moved.
#
# `--extra_execution_platforms` rather than `--host_platform`: the host platform
# keeps resolving as it does for a local build, and only remote actions pick up
# the container.
#
# No credential here. `.github/actions/remote-cache` writes the BuildBuddy
# endpoint and API key into `user.bazelrc`, which the `try-import` at the bottom
# of this file reads. On a fork, where the secret is absent, that file is never
# written and `--remote_executor` fails loudly rather than quietly building
# somewhere unexpected.
#
# `--jobs` goes far past the runner's four cores because the whole point is to
# have more actions in flight than this machine could run itself.
build:rbe --remote_executor=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io
build:rbe --extra_execution_platforms=@llvm//:rbe_linux_x86_64
build:rbe --jobs=100

# The mutation rules ship no cargo-mutants binary of their own; point them at
# the one built from //tools/mutants' lockfile.
build --@rules_rs_mutants//mutants:cargo_mutants_binary=@mutants//:cargo-mutants__cargo-mutants

# The sweep's own config. Without this the enumeration step runs unconfigured:
# it lists mutants from a synthetic workspace in a temp dir, where a
# `.cargo/mutants.toml` sitting in this one is not in scope, so the exclusions
# written there do not exclude and mutants no test can kill cannot be marked.
build --@rules_rs_mutants//mutants:config=//:.cargo/mutants.toml

# Optional developer-local overrides (registry mirrors, remote cache, etc.).
#
# CI writes this file too: `.github/actions/remote-cache` generates the
# BuildBuddy block into it on every job that runs Bazel. It cannot live here,
# because a `.bazelrc` cannot interpolate the API key out of the environment
# and a key written here is a key committed. `try-import` rather than `import`
# is what lets a fork's pull request -- which sees no secrets, so the action
# writes nothing -- build locally with no error and no `if:` guard anywhere.
try-import %workspace%/user.bazelrc
